  "response": "## Step-by-step\n\n**Given:**\n- Prevalence P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(no D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity P(–|no D) = 0.95, so P(+|no D) = 0.05\n\n**Apply Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / [P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|no D)·P(no D)]\n\n**Numerator:**\n0.98 × 0.002 = 0.00196\n\n**Denominator (total P(+)):**\n0.00196 + (0.05 × 0.998) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\n**Result:**\nP(D|+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 = 0.0377941...\n\nConverting to a percentage: 3.77941...% ≈ **3.78%**\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly good test (98% sensitivity, 95% specificity), because the condition is rare (0.2% prevalence), most positive results are false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
